

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last substantive question before I write up the design: [1mwhere the notification actually appears.[0m

  There's a trap worth naming here. "Notifications" makes people picture a thing that reaches them when the app is
  closed — a phone buzz, an email. A local-first browser app fundamentally cannot do that. True background push needs a
  server to push [3mfrom[0m; without one, nothing runs when the tab is gone. So the realistic ceiling is "visible while the
  app is open," and I'd rather you pick that knowingly than discover it at demo time.

  - [1mIn-app feed only[0m — a bell with an unread count and a dropdown list. No permission prompt, works everywhere, no
    platform quirks. The scheduler ticks while the tab is open and fills the feed; you see what fired when you next
    look.
  - [1mIn-app feed + OS notifications while the tab is open[0m — same feed, plus [38;5;153mNotification[39m API toasts that surface outside
    the browser window when you're on another app. Costs a permission prompt (which, if denied, is sticky and hard to
    recover from) and needs a graceful fallback path. Still silent when the tab is closed.
  - [1mAdd a service worker for background[0m — buys very little here: without a server there's nothing to push, and the
    periodic-sync API that could fake it is Chromium-only and throttled unpredictably. Real cost, unreliable benefit.

  [1mMy recommendation: in-app feed, with the OS-notification layer written as a thin optional adapter behind the same [0m
  [1minterface.[0m The feed is the product; OS toasts are a one-file enhancement you can switch on once the feed feels right,
  and keeping them behind an adapter means the permission mess stays in one place and never touches the scheduler.
